\title[The Multi-Window Crossing Law]{The Multi-Window Crossing Law: Step Posteriors, Layer Cakes,\\ and a Polyhedral Gate for Oracle-Guided Search}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A target is hidden in one of $M$ cells. An oracle announces $k$ nested windows $[0,w_1)\subset\cdots\subset[0,w_k)$ together with cumulative reliabilities $\alpha_1\le\cdots\le\alpha_k$. We study the expected cost of exhaustive search orders under the resulting \emph{step posterior} and ask which speedups over a fixed reference price are reachable. We prove the following.
(i) The cost of committed search equals $\sum_i(\alpha_i-\alpha_{i-1})$ times the mean slot of block $i$, which normalises to $M\Phi+\tfrac12$ for an explicit \emph{gate functional} $\Phi$.
(ii) A layer-cake displacement law expresses the cost of every order as a nonnegative combination of index sums of upper sets. Consequently committed search is Bayes-optimal among all $M!$ orders for calibrated oracles, and calibration cannot be dropped.
(iii) An exact finite gate holds: speedup $s$ is reachable if and only if $s(M\Phi+\tfrac12)\le\beta_M$, where $\beta_M=(M+1)(2M+1)/(6M)$. Its continuum limit is the polyhedral condition $\Phi\le1/(3s)$.
(iv) $\Phi=1-\AUC$, the area deficit of the piecewise-linear reliability curve.
(v) With one window the gate reduces to the classical crossing law $w\le\alpha-\alpha_{\min}(s)$, $\alpha_{\min}(s)=1-2/(3s)$.
(vi) Structural results: an outer reliability gate, an exact chord identity for refinement, convexity, and monotonicity.
We also show that the gate must include the complement block.
\end{abstract}

